WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.640
Matt Reeves' "The Batman" offers up a major
character reveal during the film's final moments,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.770
and this is your last spoiler warning before
we get into it.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:12.060
What if we told you there was supposed to
be an even bigger, more pivotal scene at Arkham

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.100
Asylum much earlier in the movie involving
this surprise prisoner that got cut?

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.310
As Reeves put it while speaking to IGN,

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.940
"The movie is not an origin tale for Batman,
but it's his early days.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.100
It really is an origin tale for the rogues
gallery."

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.160
Not only is this "origin tale" sentiment applicable
to the character we meet in the third act,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.470
but it also applies to the other antagonists
we meet earlier in the movie.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.809
Over the course of the nearly three-hour runtime
"The Batman" boasts, moviegoers are introduced

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.300
to the Riddler, Penguin, and Catwoman, followed
by the widely-talked-about Arkham reveal of

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.539
Joker, played by Barry Keoghan.

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:48.920
In Reeves' Batman universe, the character
is currently locked up for some unknown reason

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.329
and, as the director tells IGN, is only in
his early "infancy" as the Clown Prince of

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:54.329
Crime.

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:57.390
In fact, the big reveal fans get at the end
of "The Batman" was actually supposed to be

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.700
a "companion" scene to a much bigger breakout
moment involving the character at Arkham Asylum

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.729
that would have occurred earlier in the movie.

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:07.290
The scene would have featured Robert Pattinson's
Dark Knight coming to consult with the Joker

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.190
at Arkham in an attempt to profile the Riddler
in the hopes of stopping him.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.190
Reeves told IGN,

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.540
"The reason that Joker's in the movie is there
was actually another scene that was earlier.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.340
The scene that was not in the movie, the scene
that this is really the companion to…

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.110
It's a scene where Batman is so unnerved because
the Riddler is writing to him, and he figures

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:25.520
he's got to profile this killer.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.300
So, he goes to see another killer that he's
clearly had an experience with in these first

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.350
two years.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.020
And this killer in this story is not yet the
character that we come to know."

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.240
As Reeves describes it, DC villains will often
"declare their alter-egos" in response to

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.480
Batman's existence and arrival in Gotham throughout
the comics, so this was a direction that he

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:43.880
wanted to go in, too.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.120
"We have a Joker that's not yet the Joker."

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.940
As for why the scene involving Batman and
Joker was cut, the filmmaker did not say,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.230
but he did reveal what Warner Bros. has planned
for it in the future.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.520
Reeves insisted during his interview with
IGN that fans would get to see his deleted

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.280
Joker and Batman scene at some point in the
future.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.731
Whether the scene will appear in a director's
cut like "Zack Snyder's Justice League" or

00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:06.280
perhaps a Blu-ray/DVD feature of some sort
remains to be seen.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.040
"[The scene] will release at some point.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.140
In the scene that you'll see in the future,
you'll see that we worked on what he'd look

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.609
like, and he's kind of held in this kind of
very suspenseful way, away from you visually."

00:02:15.609 --> 00:02:20.060
As for what's next for the iconic DC baddie,
should he pop up again in the future is anybody's

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.060
guess.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.760
But one thing we know for sure is that Reeves
has big plans for him, telling IGN,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.480
"He's eventually going to declare himself
as a clown, declare himself as the Joker."

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.860
YouTube users who watched the interview and
heard this declaration were excited to learn

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.650
that Reeves was taking things slow but serious
with the character, especially since it gave

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.650
Paul Dano's Riddler more time to shine in
"The Batman."

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:40.650
One user wrote,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.750
"I'm actually glad they cut the scene because
if they showed him in the middle of the movie,

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:48.280
people would just think about Joker and ignore
Paul Dano's excellent Riddler performance,"

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.810
Another commenter noted how excited they were
to see a future Joker after enjoying the look

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.260
of another member of Batman's Rogues Gallery
that popped up in "The Batman,"

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.310
"Keeping him in the shadows gives them more
time to work on his look, which is being designed

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.359
by the same people who did the Penguin."

